{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And if an ox gores.</b> Whether an ox or any beast, wild animal, or bird — but Scripture spoke of the common case (Bava Kamma 54)." "<b>And its flesh shall not be eaten.</b> From the implication of what is stated, ״the ox shall be stoned״ — do I not already know that it is a nevelah, and a nevelah is forbidden in eating? So what does Scripture teach by ״and its flesh shall not be eaten״? That even if he slaughtered it after its verdict was finalized, it is forbidden in eating. From where do we derive [it is forbidden] in benefit? Scripture states: ״and the owner of the ox is clear״ — like a person who says to his fellow, So-and-so came out clean of his property and has no benefit from it whatsoever. This is its midrash (Bava Kamma 41a). And its plain sense is as it sounds — because it is stated regarding the warned ox ״and also its owner shall be put to death״, it was necessary to say regarding the innocent ox ״and the owner of the ox is clear״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:41.637865868Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}